{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And Elohim created the great sea-monsters</b> — Because of the great size of these creatures, some of them being many parasangs in length — the Greeks have stated in their books that they knew of some five hundred parasangs long, and our Sages likewise exaggerated concerning them (see Bava Batra 73b) — on this account Scripture ascribes their creation (בריאה) to Elohim, for He it is who brought them into being from nothingness at the very beginning, as I have explained the meaning of the term בריאה (Ramban on Genesis 1:1). And so too it does concerning man, in his excellence, to make known that he was brought forth from nothingness along with his intellect and understanding. And I am astonished why it does not say on this day, 'and it was so' (ויהי־כן). Perhaps it is not possible to mention 'and He created' (ויברא) after 'and it was so' (ויהי־כן), for it speaks from the first [perspective]. And our Sages said (Bava Batra 74a) that the great sea-monsters (התנינים הגדולים) are Leviathan and its mate, which He created male and female, and He slew the female and salted her for the righteous in the time to come. And it is possible that because of this it was not fitting to say concerning them 'and it was so' (ויהי־כן), for they did not endure any longer."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The phrase כי מבראשונה ידבר is rendered hyper-literally; Ramban's exact grammatical argument here is compressed and somewhat opaque, possibly meaning the verse narrates from the initial creative act forward."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-21T22:14:51.190952289Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}